WEBVTT

00:00.500 --> 00:04.942
Construct a regular hexagon
inscribed inside the circle.

00:04.942 --> 00:06.400
So what I'm going
to do, first, I'm

00:06.400 --> 00:08.169
going to draw a
diameter of the circle.

00:08.169 --> 00:10.210
And actually, I'm going
to go beyond the diameter

00:10.210 --> 00:10.793
of the circle.

00:10.793 --> 00:12.730
I'm just going to
draw a line that

00:12.730 --> 00:15.930
goes through the center of the
circle and just keeps on going.

00:15.930 --> 00:20.510
And I'm going to make it flat,
so it goes directly through,

00:20.510 --> 00:22.410
so this one right over
here, goes directly

00:22.410 --> 00:23.621
through the center.

00:23.621 --> 00:25.120
And now, what I'm
going to do is I'm

00:25.120 --> 00:26.870
going to construct
a circle that's

00:26.870 --> 00:29.350
the exact same dimensions of
this circle that's already

00:29.350 --> 00:29.950
been drawn.

00:29.950 --> 00:33.400
So let me put this
one right over here.

00:33.400 --> 00:36.952
And let me make it
the same dimensions.

00:36.952 --> 00:39.410
And now, what I'm going to do
is I'm going to move it over,

00:39.410 --> 00:41.970
so that this new
circle intersects

00:41.970 --> 00:43.430
the center of the old circle.

00:43.430 --> 00:44.960
And these circles
are the same size.

00:44.960 --> 00:48.360
So notice, this center
intersects the old circle.

00:48.360 --> 00:50.690
And the new circle
itself intersects

00:50.690 --> 00:52.550
the center of the old circle.

00:52.550 --> 00:54.630
Now, the reason why
this is interesting,

00:54.630 --> 00:56.630
we already know that this
distance, the distance

00:56.630 --> 01:00.740
between these two centers,
this is equal to a radius.

01:00.740 --> 01:04.989
We also know-- we have a
straight edge-- we also

01:04.989 --> 01:10.200
know that this distance right
over here is equal to a radius.

01:10.200 --> 01:13.570
It's equal to the radius of
our new circle right over here.

01:13.570 --> 01:18.340
We also know that this
distance right over here

01:18.340 --> 01:22.274
is equal to a radius
of our old circle.

01:22.274 --> 01:23.940
And that they both
have the same radius.

01:23.940 --> 01:26.460
So this is a radius
between these two points.

01:26.460 --> 01:28.310
Between these two
points is a radius.

01:28.310 --> 01:30.690
And then, in between these
two points is a radius.

01:30.690 --> 01:34.034
So now, I have constructed
an equilateral triangle.

01:34.034 --> 01:36.200
And essentially, you have
to just do this six times.

01:36.200 --> 01:38.650
And then I'm going to
have a hexagon inscribed

01:38.650 --> 01:39.400
inside the circle.

01:39.400 --> 01:40.910
Let me do it again.

01:40.910 --> 01:45.360
So we'll go from here to here.

01:45.360 --> 01:48.010
This is a radius of
my new circle, which

01:48.010 --> 01:51.215
is the same as the
radius of the old circle.

01:51.215 --> 01:55.848
And I could go
from here to here.

01:55.848 --> 01:57.306
That's the radius
of my old circle.

01:57.306 --> 01:58.930
So I have another
equilateral triangle.

01:58.930 --> 02:00.932
Radius, radius, radius.

02:00.932 --> 02:02.140
Another equilateral triangle.

02:02.140 --> 02:05.430
I've just got to do this, I
have to do this four more times.

02:05.430 --> 02:07.461
So let me go to my original.

02:07.461 --> 02:09.169
Let's see, let me make
sure I can-- well,

02:09.169 --> 02:11.418
it's actually going to be
hard for me to-- let me just

02:11.418 --> 02:14.750
add another circle here,
to do it on the other side.

02:14.750 --> 02:19.300
So if I put the
center of it right-- I

02:19.300 --> 02:22.470
want to move it a
little bit, right over--

02:22.470 --> 02:24.480
I want to make it the same size.

02:24.480 --> 02:27.310
So oh, that's close enough.

02:27.310 --> 02:32.820
And let's see, that
looks pretty close.

02:32.820 --> 02:34.010
That's the same size.

02:34.010 --> 02:35.450
Now, let me move it over here.

02:35.450 --> 02:40.350
Now, I want this center to be
on the circle, right like that.

02:40.350 --> 02:42.815
And now, I'm ready to draw some
more equilateral triangles.

02:46.020 --> 02:48.370
And really, I don't have to
even draw the inside of it.

02:48.370 --> 02:51.900
Now, I see my six
vertices for my hexagon,

02:51.900 --> 02:55.760
here, here, here,
here, here, here.

02:55.760 --> 02:58.980
And I think you're satisfied
now that you could break this up

02:58.980 --> 03:01.320
into six equilateral triangles.

03:01.320 --> 03:02.740
So let's do that.

03:02.740 --> 03:06.670
So this would be the base of one
of those equilateral triangles.

03:06.670 --> 03:08.890
And actually, let me move these.

03:08.890 --> 03:10.810
Let me move this
one out of the way.

03:10.810 --> 03:12.610
I can move this one
right over here.

03:12.610 --> 03:15.930
Because I really just care
about the hexagon itself.

03:15.930 --> 03:17.887
And I can move this
right over here.

03:17.887 --> 03:20.220
But we know that these are
all the lengths of the radius

03:20.220 --> 03:21.230
anyway.

03:21.230 --> 03:23.650
Actually, I'm not even having
to change the length there.

03:23.650 --> 03:26.590
Then, I have to just connect
one more right down here.

03:26.590 --> 03:30.010
So let me add another straight
edge, connect those two points.

03:30.010 --> 03:31.230
And I would have done it.

03:31.230 --> 03:38.930
I would have constructed my
regular hexagon inscribed

03:38.930 --> 03:40.750
in the circle.
